From eb143f53add311bf48ebf9c64e5e72270159386f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 20:09:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Price, gate and time-box spoken character videos
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Making character scenes speak doubles what a job costs and how long it
takes. Four consequences, handled.

PRICE. Every scene now makes a sync call on top of its image-to-video
call, so a spoken character video costs twice a narrated one: Short 8,
Medium 16, Long 24. videoJobUnits is the single function behind the
wallet reserve, the usage record and the refund, so one edit keeps all
three consistent.

DECIDED AT ENQUEUE. options.characterLipSync records the choice on the
job row rather than being read from a switch at render time. Reading it
late would let a switch flipped mid-job make the render disagree with
what was reserved and refunded.

OFF BY DEFAULT, and deliberately not a feature flag: every entry in
FEATURES defaults on, so shipping it as one would silently double the
price of every existing character video on deploy. It is a
video_gen_settings column (with a CHARACTER_LIPSYNC env fallback),
off until someone turns it on. The lipSync kill switch still applies on
top — both paths spend on the same model.

No consent gate, unlike the upload-driven engine: that one redraws a
real person's mouth, and a generated character is not anyone's
likeness. If character references ever accept uploaded photos of real
people, this is the decision to revisit.

PREFLIGHT. A character job that will sync now carries the lip_sync
engine's Replicate requirement, so it is refused before funding rather
than after generating every scene.

DEADLINE. CHARACTER_VIDEO_TOTAL_DEADLINE_MS 25 -> 35 minutes. Eight
scenes of syncing at concurrency three adds roughly 5-8; the sweep's
40-minute stuck timeout still sits above the new ceiling, which is the
constraint that matters.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
